        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Caulk Remover Actually Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            A caulk remover does not dissolve caulk. That is the single most useful thing to know before buying one, because almost every disappointed review of these products comes from someone who brushed gel on a bead, came back an hour later and expected a clean joint. What the chemicals do is soften the bead and weaken its grip on the surface so a blade can lift it. What the tools do is cut, pry and scrape. You need both on most jobs, and the right pairing depends on what the old caulk is made of.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are three chemistries in nearly every house. Acrylic latex, including the siliconised kind sold as latex plus silicone, is on painted trim, interior window casings and most of the cheaper exterior work. Silicone is in bathrooms and kitchens, and on a lot of exterior windows where somebody wanted something waterproof. Polyurethane and hybrid polymer sealants are on siding, masonry and door thresholds. A remover that is excellent on one can do almost nothing to another, and the labels are honest about this if you read the back of the tube rather than the front.
          </p>

          <p>
            The tools split the same way. Plastic and stainless hand tools are for glazed tile, porcelain, acrylic tubs and vinyl, where a scratch is permanent. Carbon steel scrapers are for wood, brick, concrete and metal, where you need force and a mark does not matter. An oscillating tool with a flexible scraper blade is for long runs, typically exterior, where hand scraping turns a morning into a weekend.
          </p>

          <p>
            This page ranks the products. If you want the full method, from scoring the bead to the final solvent wipe, our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-remove-old-caulk/">how to remove old caulk</a> covers it surface by surface. Here we focus on which bottle and which blade to buy, what each one is genuinely good at, and where each one is the wrong choice. Two of the most popular products in this category are routinely bought for jobs they were never designed for, and that is the mistake worth avoiding.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The 6 Best Caulk Removers and Removal Tools, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We compared chemical removers and removal tools on what the manufacturer labels each one for, published dwell time, which surfaces the label warns against, blade material and how well each fits the three caulk chemistries found in houses. Every figure below comes from the manufacturer's label, technical data sheet or product listing; where a figure could not be confirmed, we left it out.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
