        <div id="match-chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Match the Remover to the Caulk
          </h2>

          <p>
            The quickest identification test is the fingernail. Press into the bead. If it dents and springs back with a rubbery feel, it is silicone or a silicone hybrid. If it dents and stays dented, or feels chalky, it is acrylic latex. If it barely marks at all, it is likely cured polyurethane or a construction adhesive. Then choose a remover whose label names that chemistry, not one that simply says caulk remover in large type.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Labelled For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Published Dwell</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Avoid On</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Krud Kutter 336250</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silicone, acrylic latex, polyurethane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-20 min on dried caulk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Painted or varnished surfaces</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lift Off 41145</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silicone, latex, foam, construction adhesive</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">See bottle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plastics (per retailer warning)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DAP Caulk-Be-Gone 18026</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Latex caulks and sealants</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">At least 2 hours, max 6 per application</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silicone (not labelled for it)</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Read that table as a decision, not a ranking. If the bead is silicone around a ceramic tile tub, Krud Kutter and Lift Off are both candidates and Caulk-Be-Gone is not. If the bead is cracked latex on a painted baseboard, Caulk-Be-Gone is the right product and Krud Kutter is the wrong one, because Rust-Oleum does not recommend it on painted or varnished surfaces. If the bead is silicone on an acrylic tub, you have a narrower choice: Lift Off carries a retailer warning against plastics, so Krud Kutter, a plastic-safe hand tool and patience are the combination.
          </p>

          <p>
            Exterior polyurethane is the case where chemicals help least. Polyurethane bonds hard to porous wood and masonry, and the practical answer on most exterior jobs is a sharp knife, a carbon steel scraper or an oscillating blade, with a remover only for the thin skin left behind. Krud Kutter's label covers polyurethane, which makes it the sensible bottle for that cleanup pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you genuinely cannot tell what the bead is, buy the remover with the widest label. That is why Krud Kutter takes the top spot on this page: it is not the strongest product for every single chemistry, but it is the one least likely to be wrong when you guess.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dwell-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Dwell Time Is the Real Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            The number that matters most on a remover label is not strength, it is dwell time: how long the product has to sit before the bead is soft enough to lift. It decides whether a bathroom recaulk is a one-evening job or a two-day one, and it varies enormously across this category.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Published Dwell Times</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~1 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Krud Kutter on fresh caulk</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5-20 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Krud Kutter on dried caulk</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2 hr+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Caulk-Be-Gone minimum</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">6 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Caulk-Be-Gone max per application</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Krud Kutter's short soak is its biggest practical advantage. Rust-Oleum's directions are to saturate a dried bead and let it soak for 5 to 20 minutes until it begins to loosen, and note that some sealants need longer. On a tub surround you can work in sections, coating one wall while you scrape the last. DAP's Caulk-Be-Gone is the opposite. DAP says it works in as little as two hours, and its sheet tells you to leave it until the caulk is soft but caps a single application at six hours. That is fine for a run of baseboard you coat before lunch, and frustrating if you planned to finish the bathroom in an evening.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lift Off is the product here with the least published information on dwell. The directions are on the bottle, not on most retailer pages, so read the label before you plan the day. Treat any timing you see quoted online for it with caution unless it comes from the label itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two practical notes apply to all of them. A gel that has skinned over or dried has stopped working, so coat generously enough that it stays wet for the dwell. And dwell times are for typical room conditions; a cold garage or a hot, sunny window frame will change how fast the product works and how fast it dries out. If the bead is still firm at the end of the published time, re-coat rather than reaching for a screwdriver.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hand-tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Hand Tools: Plastic, Stainless or Carbon Steel
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every caulk removal job ends with a blade. The question is what the blade is made of, and the answer depends on the surface you are about to scrape, not on the caulk. Get it wrong and you trade a failed bead for a scratch that lasts the life of the tub.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plastic and silicone tools</h3>

          <p>
            Plastic scrapers and hooked plastic removal tools are the only safe choice on soft finishes: acrylic and fibreglass tubs and shower pans, cultured marble, vinyl window frames and painted surfaces you intend to keep. They will not cut a hard bead on their own, which is exactly why they pair best with a remover. Soften the bead, score the edges with a fresh utility blade held flat to the joint, then lift with plastic.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stainless steel tools</h3>

          <p>
            Stainless removal blades, such as the one on the Allway CT31, are the right compromise for glazed ceramic tile and porcelain. They are stiff enough to push a softened bead out of an inside corner and they do not rust in a wet room. They can still mark glass and soft finishes if you press hard, which owners of the CT31 point out in reviews, so keep the blade moving along the joint rather than levering against the tub.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carbon steel tools</h3>

          <p>
            Carbon steel, as on the Hyde Dig-It, is for hard, porous substrates outside: brick, block, concrete, bare or painted wood you plan to repaint, and metal thresholds. It takes prying, holds an edge and will scrape a hard polyurethane bead that bends a plastic tool. It will scratch anything glossy and it can rust if left wet. Buy one if your job is exterior; leave it in the garage when you are in the bathroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, a pack of fresh utility knife blades matters more than any of these. A dull blade tears and skates, and almost every gouge in a tub is a dull-blade gouge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="oscillating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When an Oscillating Tool Earns Its Place
          </h2>

          <p>
            An oscillating multi-tool is not a caulk removal tool you buy for one job. It is a tool most homeowners eventually buy for flush-cutting door casing, undercutting jambs for flooring or cutting out a section of drywall, and a flexible scraper blade turns it into the fastest way to strip a long run of exterior caulk. If you already own one, the Dremel MM610 is the cheapest upgrade on this page. If you do not, our guide to the <a href="/best-oscillating-tool/">best oscillating tools</a> covers which ones are worth owning for everything else.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case for it is length. On a single tub, hand tools and a remover are quicker than finding an extension cord. On thirty feet of window perimeters, siding butt joints or a masonry control joint around a garage, the oscillating blade does in minutes what a hand scraper does in an hour, and it does it with less wrist strain. The vibration lets the edge shear the bead off the surface rather than relying on your arm to push.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blade choice matters more than tool choice. Flexible scrapers, like the MM610, are for soft, rubbery material. Dremel recommends holding the blade at a 30 to 45 degree pitch with the blade flexed, which keeps the edge following the surface. Rigid scrapers are for hard material such as dried construction adhesive and tile thinset, and they dig if you use them on caulk against soft wood. Most people who gouge siding with an oscillating tool were using a rigid blade at too steep an angle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the interface before you order. The MM610 uses Dremel's Universal Quick-Fit, which Dremel lists for its own tools and those from Bosch, Makita, Milwaukee and Rockwell. It is not a Starlock blade, and Dremel sells a separate dual-interface version, the MM610U. And keep the tool away from glazed tile, glass and acrylic unless you have used one before; at full speed, the margin between lifting a bead and marking the surface is small.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surfaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Each Surface Will Tolerate
          </h2>

          <p>
            The surface decides the product more often than the caulk does. A remover that is perfect for silicone on ceramic tile can be the wrong choice for silicone on an acrylic tub, and a scraper that is ideal on brick will ruin vinyl. This table pairs each common surface with the remover and tool that suit it, based on the manufacturers' own label warnings.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Remover</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tool</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Watch For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ceramic tile / porcelain tub</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Krud Kutter or Lift Off</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Allway CT31, plastic scraper</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Glaze scratches from metal pressed hard</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acrylic or fibreglass tub</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Krud Kutter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plastic only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lift Off carries a no-plastics warning</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Painted interior trim</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">DAP Caulk-Be-Gone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Utility knife, plastic scraper</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Krud Kutter not recommended on paint</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Glass shower door / window</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Krud Kutter or Lift Off</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Razor held flat, plastic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stainless and carbon steel can scratch</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Brick, block, concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Krud Kutter for residue</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hyde Dig-It, Dremel MM610</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Old polyurethane needs mechanical removal</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vinyl window frame</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Krud Kutter, test first</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plastic, shallow knife angle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vinyl marks easily and shows forever</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two warnings deserve emphasis. First, the no-plastics warning on Lift Off matters because so many bathtubs and shower bases are acrylic or fibreglass, and many shower doors have plastic seals and tracks. If you are not sure what your tub is made of, tap it: acrylic and fibreglass sound hollow and feel warm, enamelled steel and cast iron sound solid and feel cold. When in doubt, treat it as plastic.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, Krud Kutter's painted-surface warning is the main reason Caulk-Be-Gone still has a place on this list. Painted trim is the one common surface where the wider-label solvent is the wrong choice and the mild latex gel is the right one. Expect to touch up paint along the edge regardless; removing trim caulk without disturbing the finish is rarely possible.
          </p>

          <p>
            For any surface you care about, test the remover on a small, hidden patch first and give it the full dwell time before you judge it. A dull patch or softened finish after twenty minutes tells you everything, and it costs a lot less than a new tub surround.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What to Buy for Your Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people need two or three items, not six. Here is the shortest list for the four most common jobs, built from the products above.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Silicone around a tiled tub or shower</h3>

          <p>
            Krud Kutter or Lift Off, an Allway CT31 and a pack of fresh utility blades. Coat the bead, score both edges, lift with the stainless blade and finish the residue with a plastic scraper. Lift Off is the better pick if the tub is enamelled steel or cast iron and there is also adhesive or foam to deal with; Krud Kutter is the safer pick if any of the surfaces are plastic. When you are ready for the new bead, our guide to the <a href="/best-bathroom-caulk/">best bathroom caulk</a> covers which silicone to put back.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cracked latex on painted baseboards and casings</h3>

          <p>
            DAP Caulk-Be-Gone, a utility knife and a plastic scraper. Coat a room's worth of joints, do something else for two hours, then score and lift. Plan to repaint the edge. The replacement should be a paintable acrylic, and our sister guide to the <a href="/best-paintable-caulk/">best paintable caulk</a> ranks the ones worth putting under trim paint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Old polyurethane on exterior windows and siding</h3>

          <p>
            A utility knife, a Hyde Dig-It and, if you own an oscillating tool, a Dremel MM610. Cut through any paint film along both edges first so you do not peel a strip of finish off the siding. Use Krud Kutter only for the thin skin left on bare masonry or metal. For the replacement sealant, see our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">best exterior caulk</a> rankings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Glass shower doors</h3>

          <p>
            Krud Kutter or Lift Off, a single-edge razor held almost flat on the glass and a plastic scraper for the frame. Keep the steel tools off the glass and check the door's seals and tracks before using Lift Off near them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Isn't Worth Buying
          </h2>

          <p>
            The caulk removal aisle, and especially its online version, is full of products that look like a solution and are not. A few categories are worth avoiding on principle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Generic adhesive removers sold as caulk removers. Citrus adhesive removers and sticker lifters are good at what they are made for, which is tape residue, labels and gum. They are not labelled for cured silicone, and buying one because it appeared in a caulk search is how people end up scraping a hard bead with a softened film on top. If the back of the bottle does not name silicone, latex or polyurethane, it is not a caulk remover.
          </p>

          <p>
            Large no-name multi-tool kits. Online listings sell five-in-one and seven-piece caulking kits with interchangeable heads, spatulas and pads. Some are fine, but most have thin blades that flex and shallow plastic that cracks, and you will use one or two of the pieces. A single well-made tool like the Allway CT31, plus fresh utility blades, does the same job with fewer failure points.
          </p>

          <p>
            Latex removers for silicone. This is worth repeating because it is the most common mistake in the category. DAP Caulk-Be-Gone is a good product for latex, and the name tempts people to use it on a shower. DAP's own sheet describes it as a latex remover. Buying it for silicone costs you the price of the tube and the two hours you waited.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat guns. Heat softens some latex and does little useful to silicone, and it is genuinely risky around acrylic tubs, vinyl frames and painted trim. A heat gun is a paint-stripping tool; it has no place in a caulk removal kit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. After the Remover: Residue and the New Bead
          </h2>

          <p>
            A remover gets the bulk of the bead out. It does not leave the joint ready for new caulk on its own. Silicone in particular leaves a thin film that new sealant will not bond to, and the remover itself has to be rinsed or wiped off according to its label before anything new goes on. This is the step that decides whether the next bead lasts ten years or ten months.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the cleanup itself, the order is: scrape the film with a plastic blade held almost flat, abrade lightly with a non-scratch pad, rinse or wipe off the remover as its label directs, then do a final wipe with isopropyl alcohol and let it dry completely. A water mist test tells you whether you are done: water should wet the surface evenly rather than bead up. The full method, including the drying wait on porous surfaces, is in our <a href="/how-to-remove-old-caulk/">caulk removal guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are tempted to skip removal entirely and caulk over the top, read our answer to <a href="/can-you-caulk-over-caulk/">can you caulk over caulk</a> first. It occasionally works with latex over latex on painted trim, and almost never works with anything over silicone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then match the new bead to the joint. Bathroom and kitchen joints that see water want a mould-resistant silicone. Painted interior trim wants a paintable acrylic or siliconised acrylic. Exterior joints that move want an elastomeric or hybrid sealant. Our <a href="/silicone-vs-latex-caulk/">silicone vs latex caulk</a> comparison explains the trade-offs, and a decent <a href="/best-caulk-gun/">caulk gun</a> makes the new bead far easier to lay neatly than a cheap one does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a latex remover for silicone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                DAP Caulk-Be-Gone is the best-known remover on the shelf and its name suggests it removes any caulk. DAP's technical data sheet describes it as a latex caulk remover. On a silicone shower bead it will not do the job, so check the bead with a fingernail first and buy a remover whose label names silicone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging the remover before the dwell time is up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Removers soften; they do not dissolve. Coming back after ten minutes to a two-hour gel, finding a firm bead and scraping harder is how tubs get gouged. Give the product its full published time, keep it wet, and re-coat a stubborn section rather than reaching for a screwdriver or chisel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using metal tools on acrylic and fibreglass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stainless and carbon steel blades leave permanent marks on acrylic tubs, fibreglass shower pans, cultured marble and vinyl. On those surfaces use plastic scrapers and a utility blade held flat to the joint, and let the chemical remover do more of the work than you would on tile or brick.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the label's surface warnings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Krud Kutter is not recommended on painted or varnished surfaces, and Lift Off listings warn against plastics. Those warnings exist because the products can soften or dull those finishes. Read the back of the bottle before you coat a whole wall, and test on a hidden patch first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recaulking straight after removal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The remover and the old silicone film both have to come off before new caulk will bond. Rinse or wipe as the label directs, abrade, wipe with alcohol and let the joint dry fully. Laying new caulk over remover residue is the most reliable way to have it peel within months.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best caulk remover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, Krud Kutter Caulk Remover 336250, because Rust-Oleum labels it for silicone, acrylic latex and polyurethane, and its published soak on dried caulk is 5 to 20 minutes. The exceptions are painted trim, where Krud Kutter is not recommended and DAP Caulk-Be-Gone is the better choice, and jobs with construction adhesive or foam, where Lift Off covers more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does DAP Caulk-Be-Gone work on silicone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                DAP's technical data sheet describes Caulk-Be-Gone as a remover for latex caulks and sealants, and it does not claim to work on silicone. For a silicone bead in a bathroom or kitchen, choose a product labelled for silicone, such as Krud Kutter Caulk Remover or Motsenbocker's Lift Off Foam and Caulk Remover, and pair it with a plastic or stainless hand tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best silicone caulk remover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Krud Kutter and Lift Off are both labelled for silicone. Krud Kutter is the safer pick when the tub, pan or frame is plastic, since Lift Off listings warn against plastics. Lift Off is the better pick when the job also includes construction adhesive, polyurethane glue or expanding foam, on tile, glass, stone or metal surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best caulk removal tool?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For bathrooms and kitchens, the Allway CT31 3-in-1 Caulk Tool, which pairs a stainless removal blade with a silicone smoothing head for the new bead. For hard exterior beads on brick, wood and metal, the Hyde Dig-It's carbon steel blade. For long exterior runs, a Dremel MM610 flexible scraper blade on an oscillating multi-tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I remove caulk without chemicals?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and on many jobs that is the normal approach. Latex often lifts with a sharp utility knife and a scraper alone, and old exterior polyurethane usually needs mechanical removal regardless. Chemicals help most with silicone, which grips glazed surfaces hard, and with residue. Fresh utility blades matter more than any single remover or tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an oscillating tool good for removing caulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On long exterior runs, yes. With a flexible scraper blade such as the Dremel MM610, held at the 30 to 45 degree pitch Dremel recommends, it strips window, siding and masonry joints much faster than hand tools. It is too aggressive for glazed tile, glass and acrylic in inexperienced hands, and on a single tub it is rarely worth setting up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should caulk remover sit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends entirely on the product. Krud Kutter's directions call for 5 to 20 minutes on dried caulk and about a minute on fresh. DAP Caulk-Be-Gone needs at least two hours, and DAP's sheet caps one application at six. Lift Off's directions are on the bottle. Keep the product wet for the whole dwell and re-coat if needed.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

