        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Identify the bead before you buy anything. If it is silicone, buy Krud Kutter or Lift Off and an Allway CT31; if it is latex on painted trim, DAP Caulk-Be-Gone and a plastic scraper; if it is old polyurethane outside, a Hyde Dig-It and, if you own an oscillating tool, a Dremel MM610. For a guess, Krud Kutter's wide label and short soak make it the safest single bottle.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then read the back of the bottle for the surface warnings, give the product its full dwell, and clean the joint properly before the new bead goes on. The remover is the easy part of a recaulk; the residue and the replacement caulk decide how long the job lasts. Our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">exterior caulk guide</a> covers what goes back on the outside of the house.
          </p>
